{"name":"Jester Blue","hex":"#216879","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jester","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One who jests, jokes or teases.","A person in colourful garb and fool's cap who amused a medieval and early modern royal or noble court."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To act in a foolish or clownish way for the purpose of entertainment or attracting attention."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jester"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jester","blocks":[{"text":"A jester, also known as joker, court jester, or fool, was a member of the household of a nobleman or a monarch kept to entertain guests at the royal court. Jesters were also travelling performers who entertained common folk at fairs and town markets, and the discipline continues into the modern day, where jesters perform at historical-themed events. Jester-like figures were common throughout the world, including ancient Rome, China, Persia, the Aztec Empire and Medieval Europe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Heinrich_Vogtherr_d._J._Schalksnarr.JPG/330px-Heinrich_Vogtherr_d._J._Schalksnarr.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jester","wikidata":"Q215548"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Fool_or_jester_playing_a_dog_shaped_bagpipe%2C_image_by_Petrus_Gilberti%2C_from_Bible_Historiale_%28Royal_15_D11_folio_262%29.jpg/500px-Fool_or_jester_playing_a_dog_shaped_bagpipe%2C_image_by_Petrus_Gilberti%2C_from_Bible_Historiale_%28Royal_15_D11_folio_262%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Fool_or_jester_playing_a_dog_shaped_bagpipe%2C_image_by_Petrus_Gilberti%2C_from_Bible_Historiale_%28Royal_15_D11_folio_262%29.jpg/500px-Fool_or_jester_playing_a_dog_shaped_bagpipe%2C_image_by_Petrus_Gilberti%2C_from_Bible_Historiale_%28Royal_15_D11_folio_262%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1404–1425, France. Two men smile at a jester's act, appearing to use a dog for a bagpipe.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFool_or_jester_playing_a_dog_shaped_bagpipe%2C_image_by_Petrus_Gilberti%2C_from_Bible_Historiale_(Royal_15_D11_folio_262).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Schuttersfeest%2C_Meester_van_Frankfurt%2C_%281493%29%2C_Koninklijk_Museum_voor_Schone_Kunsten_Antwerpen%2C_529.jpg/500px-Schuttersfeest%2C_Meester_van_Frankfurt%2C_%281493%